## 3. AI fraud detection

0 of 15 complete

P0

Choose a tabular ML model such as CatBoost or XGBoost for the first version.

Build a reproducible training and evaluation pipeline.

Use a real public fraud dataset such as ULB Credit Card Fraud Detection for a general fraud benchmark.

Document the dataset's origin, labels, limitations, class imbalance and permitted use.

Create a separate offline-payment-specific dataset of legitimate and suspicious scenarios.

Include features available at settlement time: transaction amount, offline duration, velocity, spending ratio and token-claim conflicts where observable.

Prevent data leakage from future events, labels or post-settlement information.

Split real data into training, validation and held-out test sets, preferably respecting time order.

Keep mandatory signature, expiry, reservation and spending-limit checks deterministic.

Expose an ML inference endpoint that returns a risk score and model version.

Integrate the model with the existing Spring Boot Fraud Service.

Persist the transaction ID, score, model version, reason codes and assessment timestamp.

Define risk bands and a documented settlement, hold or review policy.

Ensure the AI cannot override mandatory security failures.

Run the model in shadow mode before allowing it to affect settlement decisions.

