## 5. Security, ledger integrity and double-spending

0 of 14 complete

P0

Validate signed tokens using the correct issuer key and algorithm configuration.

Keep session authentication keys separate from offline-token signing keys.

Never trust amount, user identity or settlement status supplied only by the frontend.

Prevent duplicate processing of the same transaction ID.

Test two different transaction IDs that claim the same token or spending allowance.

Track cumulative claims against the authorized offline allowance during synchronization.

Define a conflict policy for transactions that collectively exceed the allowance.

Use database constraints and transactions to protect ledger invariants.

Make funding and settlement operations idempotent.

Handle Kafka retries, duplicate events and failed batches safely.

Use recoverable state transitions for PayPal capture and local ledger credit because they are not one atomic transaction.

Protect secrets, credentials, signing keys and sensitive data.

Document that post-reconnection AI can detect suspicious spending but cannot retroactively prevent goods from being handed over offline.

Document the remaining double-spend risks and any prevention mechanisms that are actually implemented.

